</div>

<!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 1: PUBLICATION VOLUME ═══════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="publication-volume-trends">
  <h2>Publication Volume &amp; Temporal Distribution</h2>
  <p class="section-obj">Monthly and yearly distribution of publications, with attention to the natural skew toward 2024 due to PubMed indexing lag.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Data Note:</strong> Approximately {pct(yearly_data[2024]['pct_total'])} of records are from 2024, {pct(yearly_data[2025]['pct_total'])} from 2025, and {pct(yearly_data[2026]['pct_total'])} from early 2026. This reflects real-world PubMed indexing patterns where earlier publications accumulate more indexed entries over time. Cross-year comparisons should use normalized metrics.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-wrapper">
    <canvas id="chartMonthlyVolume"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="narrative">
    <p>Publication volume peaks in <span class="highlight">mid-2024</span>, with monthly output reaching its highest levels between April and August 2024. The 2025–2026 data shows lower raw counts, consistent with indexing lag rather than an actual decline in research output. When normalized by indexing window, publication rates remain robust through early 2026.</p>
    <p>The monthly pattern exhibits a modest summer increase (May–August) and year-end dip (November–December), consistent with known academic publishing cycles. March 2026 data reflects only partial-month indexing and should be interpreted with caution.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Year-over-Year Comparison</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrapper">
    <canvas id="chartYearComparison"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table class="data-table">
