    <thead>
      <tr><th>Country</th><th>Publications</th><th>Share</th><th>OA Rate</th><th>Median Authors</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
"""
for i, (country, cnt) in enumerate(top10_countries.items()):
    share = cnt / total_pubs * 100
    oa = country_oa.get(country, 0)
    auth = country_median_authors.get(country, 0)
    HTML += f"<tr><td>{i+1}. {country}</td><td>{fmt(cnt)}</td><td>{pct(share)}</td><td>{pct(oa)}</td><td>{auth:.0f}</td></tr>\n"

HTML += """
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="narrative">
    <p>The <span class="highlight">United States and China</span> dominate biomedical research output, together accounting for a substantial share of publications. The top 10 countries collectively produce <span class="highlight">""" + pct(top10_share) + """</span> of all indexed research. Open access rates vary significantly by country, with some nations showing markedly higher OA adoption than others.</p>
    <p>Notable geographic patterns include strong representation from Western Europe (UK, Germany, Italy, France), East Asia (China, Japan, South Korea), and South Asia (India). Emerging research nations in the Middle East and Southeast Asia show growing but still modest presence.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 3: TOPIC & MESH ANALYSIS ═══════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="topic-and-mesh-analysis">
  <h2>Research Topic &amp; MeSH Keyword Analysis</h2>
  <p class="section-obj">Prevalent major MeSH topics, trending research areas, and topic shifts between years.</p>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-wrapper">
      <canvas id="chartTopics"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper">
      <canvas id="chartTopicGrowth"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="narrative">
    <p>Traditional biomedical pillars — <span class="highlight">Humans</span>, <span class="highlight">Animals</span>, and <span class="highlight">Female/Male</span> demographic terms — remain the most prevalent MeSH major topics, reflecting their role as standard indexing descriptors. Beyond these, disease-focused topics like <span class="highlight">Neoplasms</span>, <span class="highlight">COVID-19</span>, and <span class="highlight">Obesity</span> represent the largest content-driven clusters.</p>
    <p>The <span class="highlight-green">fastest-growing topics</span> indicate areas of accelerating research investment. <strong>""" + growth_names[0] + """</strong> leads with an average """ + pct(growth_vals[0]) + """ month-over-month growth rate, followed by <strong>""" + growth_names[1] + """</strong> (""" + pct(growth_vals[1]) + """). These emerging areas merit attention from funding strategists.</p>
  </div>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Major Topic</th><th>Publications</th><th>Avg MoM Growth</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
"""
for i, (topic, cnt) in enumerate(top_topics.head(20).items()):
    growth = topic_growth.get(topic, 0)
    g_class = "highlight-green" if growth > 0 else "highlight-warn"
    HTML += f"<tr><td>{i+1}</td><td>{topic}</td><td>{fmt(cnt)}</td><td class='{g_class}'>{growth:+.1f}%</td></tr>\n"

HTML += """
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 4: OPEN ACCESS ═══════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="open-access-patterns">
  <h2>Open Access Adoption &amp; Journal Landscape</h2>
  <p class="section-obj">Open-access vs. closed-access proportions, temporal OA trends, and variation by country and research type.</p>

